<section class="page-hero"><img class="bg" src="/assets/img/photos/living-room.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/living-room-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/living-room.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="800" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.1"><div class="wrap"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span>Insurance appraisal FAQ</nav><p class="kicker lt">Answers by topic</p><h1>Insurance Appraisal FAQ for South Carolina Owners</h1><p class="lede">Insurance appraisal FAQ, grouped by topic: the basics, costs, timing, South Carolina specifics and how we work.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn cu" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn ol" href="/contact-an-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><div class="note"><b class="h">Jump to a topic</b><ul style="margin:6px 0 0"><li><a href="#faq-1">The basics</a></li><li><a href="#faq-2">Costs &amp; fees</a></li><li><a href="#faq-3">Timing &amp; deadlines</a></li><li><a href="#faq-4">South Carolina specifics</a></li><li><a href="#faq-5">Working with us</a></li></ul></div><h2 id="faq-1">The basics</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>What does an insurance appraiser actually do?</summary><div class="a"><p>An insurance appraiser values the cost to repair or replace damaged property under the terms of a policy. When you and your insurer agree a loss is covered but disagree on the price, each side names an appraiser, and those two try to agree on the amount of loss, with an umpire breaking any deadlock.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is appraisal the same as going to court?</summary><div class="a"><p>No. Appraisal is a private process written into your policy, and it normally decides only the amount of loss. A lawsuit can address coverage, contract interpretation and other legal claims. For anything beyond price, talk with a South Carolina attorney.</p></div></details><details><summary>Who can invoke appraisal?</summary><div class="a"><p>Either party: you or the insurance company. The demand should be in writing, cite the appraisal provision in your policy and state that the parties disagree on the amount of loss.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is the appraisal award binding?</summary><div class="a"><p>On the amount of loss, generally yes, once any two of the three panel members sign. Courts can set awards aside in limited situations, such as fraud, a biased panel member or a panel deciding questions outside its authority.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does appraisal work for commercial and HOA claims?</summary><div class="a"><p>Yes. Commercial, condominium association and HOA master policies usually carry an appraisal clause that works the same way. Those claims often involve more buildings, more trades and contents or business income, so they tend to take longer.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="faq-2">Costs &amp; fees</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>How are insurance appraisers paid?</summary><div class="a"><p>Arrangements vary. Some appraisers bill hourly, some charge a flat fee and some work for a percentage of the award or of the increase over the insurer’s number. Whatever the structure, get it in writing before you sign.</p></div></details><details><summary>Who pays the umpire?</summary><div class="a"><p>Under most policy wording, each side pays its own appraiser and the two sides share the umpire’s fee equally. Check your policy, because some carriers use different language.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is appraisal worth it for a small gap?</summary><div class="a"><p>Often not. If the difference between estimates is only a few thousand dollars, appraisal fees can eat most of the gain. A well-documented supplement request or a call to the adjuster’s supervisor may be the better first step.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can I recover appraisal costs from the insurer?</summary><div class="a"><p>Usually the policy says each side bears its own appraisal costs. Whether any fees can be shifted in a later dispute is a legal question that depends on the facts, so ask a South Carolina attorney rather than assuming either way.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="faq-3">Timing &amp; deadlines</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>How long does appraisal take in South Carolina?</summary><div class="a"><p>Many residential appraisals wrap up within two to four months of the demand, depending on how fast both appraisers are named and whether an umpire is needed. Large commercial, condo or contents claims, and periods right after a major hurricane, can stretch that timeline.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is there a deadline to demand appraisal?</summary><div class="a"><p>Some policies set one and many don’t, but waiting too long can create arguments about waiver. Separately, your policy may set a deadline to file suit, and South Carolina contract claims generally carry a three-year limitation period. Confirm with a South Carolina attorney how those rules apply to your claim.</p></div></details><details><summary>How quickly must each side name its appraiser?</summary><div class="a"><p>Many policies give each side 20 days from the demand to name an appraiser and give the appraisers 15 days to agree on an umpire. Calendar those dates the day the demand goes out or arrives.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does appraisal pause my deadline to sue?</summary><div class="a"><p>Not automatically. Depending on the policy wording and the facts, the suit-limitation clock may keep running during appraisal. If your deadline is getting close, speak with a South Carolina attorney before relying on appraisal alone.</p></div></details><details><summary>When does the insurer have to pay after an award?</summary><div class="a"><p>The policy usually sets a payment window after the award is signed, often measured in days. Expect the deductible and prior payments to come off, recoverable depreciation to be held until repairs are complete, and your mortgage lender to be named on the check.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="faq-4">South Carolina specifics</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>How do hurricane deductibles work in South Carolina?</summary><div class="a"><p>Many coastal policies use a hurricane or named-storm deductible set as a percentage of the dwelling limit, often 1% to 5% or more, instead of a flat dollar amount. Whether it applies depends on the trigger wording in your policy, such as a watch or warning being issued. Check the declarations page, where the deductible must be disclosed.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does a wind pool policy from SCWHUA include appraisal?</summary><div class="a"><p>The South Carolina Wind and Hail Underwriting Association insures wind and hail in designated coastal areas, and its policies, like most property forms, generally contain an appraisal provision. Read your current SCWHUA policy for the exact wording, and remember that your homeowners and flood policies are handled separately.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can flood damage go to appraisal?</summary><div class="a"><p>Flood is excluded from standard homeowners policies and is covered by a separate NFIP or private flood policy. The NFIP’s standard flood policy has its own appraisal provision and strict proof-of-loss rules set by federal regulation. Private flood policies follow their own wording.</p></div></details><details><summary>Where can I complain about how my claim was handled?</summary><div class="a"><p>The South Carolina Department of Insurance (SCDOI) accepts consumer complaints and licenses adjusters. A complaint can prompt a response from the carrier, but SCDOI doesn’t set the value of your loss. Questions about bad faith or unfair claims practices are for a South Carolina attorney.</p></div></details><details><summary>What happens if the appraisers can’t agree on an umpire?</summary><div class="a"><p>Most policies let either side ask a court of record in the county where the property is located to appoint one. In South Carolina, that is the circuit court for the county, and the request is a short filing that an attorney typically handles.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="faq-5">Working with us</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>What do you need to review my claim?</summary><div class="a"><p>Send the full policy and declarations page, the insurer’s estimate, any contractor estimates, correspondence with the adjuster and your photos. With those, we can tell you whether the gap looks like an amount dispute that suits appraisal.</p></div></details><details><summary>Are you a public adjuster or a law firm?</summary><div class="a"><p>Neither. We are an independent insurance appraisal practice. We serve as appraiser, estimate reviewer or umpire on disputed property claims. We don’t negotiate the claim as a public adjuster would, and we don’t give legal advice.</p></div></details><details><summary>Do you serve my part of South Carolina?</summary><div class="a"><p>We work on claims statewide, from the Upstate and Midlands to the Pee Dee, the Grand Strand and the Lowcountry. Inspections are scheduled around the claim, and much of the estimate work can be done from your documents.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can you act as an umpire?</summary><div class="a"><p>Yes, when both appraisers agree to appoint us or a court does. As an umpire, we stay neutral and decide only the items the two appraisers can’t resolve.</p></div></details><details><summary>Will you tell me if appraisal isn’t a good fit?</summary><div class="a"><p>Yes. If the gap is small, the dispute is really about coverage, or a legal deadline is close, we’ll say so plainly and suggest other routes, including speaking with a South Carolina attorney.</p></div></details></div></article>
